Subject: [PATCH 10857/11171] Record a FAIL message that names the wrong cause

Today's three FAILs are one task, exp6922, at 05:38, 05:54 and 06:09Z -- the
3-strike pattern that retires an experiment. The artifact is complete, carries a
terminal verdict, and passed all five of its gate checks. It was written at
06:05Z, before the third FAIL.

Reproduced: _artifact_is_finished returns False for it right now, while status
is 'complete' and not in _BOOTSTRAP_STATUSES. The status branch is not what
fires. The second gate is, and it is not the verdict string either --
_verdict_is_untrustworthy returns True for the full artifact and False for the
same verdict string passed alone. The artifact declares verdict_class partial
while its honest_verdict opens with the terminal prefix complete_, and the
classifier resolves that contradiction against the prefix.

Distrusting a self-contradictory artifact is defensible. What is not:

The FAIL message says artifact_not_updated_past_bootstrap, which asserts the
artifact was never written. It was written and it is complete.
_artifact_is_finished has two failure paths and _log_experiment_completion emits
the bootstrap message for both. An hour of this morning's investigation went
into the wrong hypothesis because of that string.

And adversarial_verify detects the same contradiction as a WARN. Two checkers
see one defect; one shrugs while the other burns three attempts and retires the
task. If it is severe enough to act on, the artifact-side check should block at
write time rather than let the loop discover it three runs later.

Not fixed here: changing a warn to a block changes what commits are refused, and
splitting a FAIL message changes what the log means. Both are operator calls.
Records the fix shape instead.

Explicitly not a regression of REQ-CONDUCTOR-FINISHED-1, which covers the
blocked-status path and is live. Base rate checked before writing any of this:
FAIL counts for the last eight days are 14, 14, 8, 22, 17, 8, 6, 3, so today is
the lowest of the eight and this is not a volume regression.

